WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:06.080
Iran and America have been bitter rivals for decades

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.720
Iranian lawmakers chanted ‘‘death to America’’

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.760
241 US marines died in a separate suicide-bombing

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.960
There have been sanctions…

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.360
…hostage-taking…

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.000
…proxy wars…

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.720
…and assassinations

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.880
Iran is constantly on the agenda

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.320
These have shaped…

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.640
…not just the relationship between Iran and America…

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.640
…but much of the Middle East

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.760
The rivalry has largely defined the region…

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.400
…for the past four decades

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.640
So what’s behind the feud?

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.720
And could President Biden…

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.000
…help ease decades of tension?

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.280
If you want to understand…

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.840
…Iran’s troubled relationship with America…

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.760
…you need to go back to the 1950s…

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.040
...when it all started in a fight over oil

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.560
At the time Iran was home to one of the biggest oil refineries…

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.560
…in the world—Abadan

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.120
But most Iranians didn’t benefit from this vast wealth…

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.000
…as the oil fields were controlled by the British

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.360
A British employee showing Persians how to shut off the flow of oil…

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:21.960
…now that the tanks are full

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.400
This angered many in Iran…

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.720
…and in 1951 Iran nationalised the oil industry

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.280
The campaign was led…

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.360
…by a nationalist leader, Mohammad Mosaddegh…

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.200
…who became prime minister

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.640
When Mosaddegh came and tried to nationalise the oil…

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.000
…Britain considered this theft

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:45.000
The concern was this…

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.400
…if they allow Iran to nationalise its oil…

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.880
…what is to prevent Iraq, Saudi Arabia and others…

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.520
…to follow suit and do the same?

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.840
For years Britain tried diplomacy to win back the oil

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.000
But talking didn’t work

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.360
The British needed something more radical…

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.280
…and approached America for help 

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.320
As these CIA documents show…

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.120
…the British persuaded the Americans…

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.360
…to join them in a plot

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.920
Together they would remove Mosaddegh…

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.680
...and in doing so, change the course of history

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.360
British and American spies stirred up violence in Iran…

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.040
…and Mosaddegh was swept from power

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.560
That experience really was a watershed event…

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.080
…in the modern history of Iran

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.440
With Mosaddegh gone…

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:37.960
…the Americans needed a leader…

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:39.920
…they could more easily control

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:46.320
So in 1953 they helped reinstall the country’s king—the shah

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.000
Iran was an important strategic ally for America…

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.360
…as it provided a buffer against the Soviet Union

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.760
And the Americans hoped the shah would provide access…

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.120
…to cheap oil

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:04.880
Iran, because of the great leadership of the shah…

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.520
…is an island of stability…

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.560
…in one of the more troubled areas of the world

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.600
But America’s support could not protect the shah…

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.040
…from problems at home

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.880
The shah was perceived as somebody…

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.360
…who had been reinstated in power…

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.680
…thanks to this coup

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.280
So as you can imagine…

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.040
…he was suffering from a crisis of legitimacy

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.000
At the appointed moment, the shah of Persia will arrive

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.120
The shah was hell-bent on Westernising Iran

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.480
He introduced sweeping reforms…

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.760
…that increased access to education…

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.720
…and gave women the vote 

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.760
But some thought the reforms went too far

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.560
Eventually that Western influence…

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.000
…would anger a lot of people, including the Shia clergy

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.040
And then you had the rest of the population…

00:03:59.040 --> 00:03:59.840
…which was just angry…

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.200
…with the way the shah sort of handles his business…

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.440
…he was extravagant, he was corrupt…

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.200
…he cracked down on any dissent

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.800
It was becoming clear the shah’s days were numbered

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.440
Protesters filled the streets in violent clashes…

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.200
…with the authorities

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:23.960
There was one man…

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.920
…who embodied the revolutionary movement

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:30.040
The exiled Shia cleric Ayatollah Khomeini

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:35.880
He returned to Iran from Paris in February 1979

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.880
And was greeted like a messiah

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.600
His return would give the revolution…

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.160
…the final push it needed

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.600
The revolution really came like a sudden tsunami

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.400
It was unexpected

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.320
And it happened in such a short time span…

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.320
…that not even the Ayatollah and his lieutenants…

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.160
…expected, you know, such a quick victory

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.960
Within a few months, Iran had been declared an Islamic Republic…

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.640
…which it remains to this day

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.480
This was a bitter blow for America

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.320
But things were about to get much worse

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.840
The shah had left Iran before Khomeini returned…

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:21.800
…and months later…

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:25.000
…President Jimmy Carter allowed him to come to America…

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.520
…for cancer treatment…

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.560
…which caused outrage in Iran…

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.240
…and paved the way…

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.000
…for one of the worst geopolitical fiascos…

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.920
…America had ever suffered

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.720
The Iran crisis. America held hostage

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.720
Landmark reports like this broke the news to the nation

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.160
The US embassy in Tehran…

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.960
…has been invaded and occupied by Iranian students

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.840
Angry that the shah had escaped justice…

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.800
…students took a group of US embassy employees hostage…

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.080
…demanding that the shah return to stand trial

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.640
The hostages were regularly paraded in front of the cameras

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:10.720
Look at this, one American, blindfolded, handcuffed

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.560
That hostage crisis really traumatised…

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.040
…the American political establishment…

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:21.360
…and made Iran into a very prominent nemesis

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.080
It became the poster-child…

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:27.280
…for what we refer to nowadays as Islamic fundamentalism

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.920
The crisis played out over 444 days

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.080
A rescue attempt went wrong…

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:37.000
…and Jimmy Carter failed to win re-election

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:41.880
Meanwhile, trouble was brewing on the country’s western border

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:45.440
Iraq’s leader, Saddam Hussein, had taken advantage…

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:48.960
…of the political upheaval to invade Khuzestan…

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.560
…home to the Abadan oil refinery 

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:58.200
The Iranians retaliated and it turned into a gruelling eight-year war

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:06.800
America supplied weapons to both sides…

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.800
…prolonging the conflict…

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.560
…but it mainly tried to sway the balance of power against Iran

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.360
For America, it was a chance to really sort of stick it to the Iranians

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:20.960
So they backed up Saddam Hussein…

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:21.920
…did everything they could…

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.120
…to sort of tilt the war slightly to his advantage

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.240
Iran and Iraq attacked each other’s ships

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:33.760
So the American navy started to escort oil tankers in the Gulf

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:35.360
And America soon became drawn…

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.080
…into direct fighting with Iranian forces 

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:39.840
In one incident…

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:41.040
…an American warship…

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:43.920
…shot an Iranian passenger plane out of the sky…

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:47.920
…whilst under attack—mistaking it for a fighter jet

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.000
Both Iran and Iraq suffered huge losses…

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.960
…but, on balance, Iraq came out on top

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.440
The conflict hardened Iranian attitudes towards America…

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.640
…which still shape Iran’s politics today

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:05.960
Iran’s current political elite…

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.760
…their baptism of fire happened during that Iran-Iraq war

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.560
The formative years of their lives were spent in the war front…

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.240
…so you cannot now blame them…

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:19.600
…for having this security-minded outlook on things

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:24.840
The Iran-Iraq war was only one in a series of proxy wars…

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.880
…where America was pitted against Iran

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.600
Another was in Lebanon

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:34.680
Israel—a friend of the West…

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.880
…invaded Lebanon in 1982…

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:41.720
…to push out the Palestine Liberation Organisation, or PLO

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.000
Iran intervened in the war to help arm and train…

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:49.760
…a radical Shia militia

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:53.560
Iran doesn’t have the military might…

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:56.520
…to fight the US or Israel

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.120
The only way Iran can have an advantage…

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.320
…is through asymmetrical warfare

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:06.400
And this is where proxy groups become handy

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:13.920
America and other Western countries were drawn into the war…

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.960
…and Iran-backed suicide-bombers…

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:18.920
…attacked Israeli and Western targets…

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.840
…including the US embassy…

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:28.040
…and a marine base in Beirut—where hundreds of Americans were killed 

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:33.520
It was yet another trauma caused by Iran on America…

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:36.360
…it sort of went without retaliation

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.040
So it really showed up America’s weakness

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.520
At the time, the militant group behind the bombing…

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.760
…didn’t operate under one name…

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.320
…but it soon became known as Hizbullah or ‘‘The Party of God’’

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:54.560
It is both a militia and a political party…

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:57.120
…and dominates Lebanon today

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.440
Throughout the 1980s and 1990s, Hizbullah and other proxy groups…

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.840
…carried out multiple attacks on US…

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.320
…and especially Israeli targets around the world

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:09.160
But then everything changed

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:15.000
Iran had nothing to do with 9/11

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:21.080
But Iran came to experience the repercussions of the 9/11 attacks

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:22.080
Oh my God

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:25.040
I’ve never seen anything like it in my life

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:29.080
By now, Iraq under Saddam Hussein…

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.760
…had also become a foe of America’s

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:33.520
In the wake of the attacks…

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.400
…America invaded the country to depose him

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:44.240
This was a crucial moment in America’s relationship with Iran

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.760
And with Americans on their doorstep…

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.120
…Iran accelerated its nuclear programme

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:52.880
And America was worried

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:55.760
Iran had been working to develop missiles

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:57.480
The nuclear threat from Iran…

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.080
…was sort of front and centre at this point

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:08.280
Iran claimed its nuclear ambitions were for civilian purposes…

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:10.560
…not for weapons

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:11.840
But the West responded…

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:14.880
…by imposing ever tighter sanctions

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:17.520
The sanctions didn’t stop the country…

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.280
…building centrifuges to enrich uranium…

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:25.960
…which went from zero in 2006 to almost 20,000…

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:27.600
… seven years later 

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:28.680
But sanctions…

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.160
…particularly those on Iran’s oil exports…

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:33.240
…did cripple Iran’s economy…

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.040
…pushing its leaders to the negotiating table

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:39.240
And for the first time since the hostage crisis…

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:42.000
…America, too, was willing to negotiate

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:45.000
President Barack Obama’s administration…

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:48.800
…negotiated a deal with Iran’s President Hassan Rouhani

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:51.240
…called the Joint Comprehensive Plan Of Action…

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.240
…or JCPOA

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:00.120
There were two moderate leaders in each country…

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:01.320
…that both realised, you know…

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.000
…you cannot start from maximalist demands…

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:07.000
…and expect the other one to surrender entirely

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.360
The deal was groundbreaking

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:12.280
It limited Iran’s nuclear facilities…

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:14.960
…and opened it up to more rigorous inspections…

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:17.680
…in return for sanctions relief

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:20.800
But not everyone saw it as a success

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:26.920
Never, ever, in my life have I seen any transaction…

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:31.160
…so incompetently negotiated as our deal with Iran

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:35.720
President Donald Trump withdrew from the deal in 2018…

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:41.520
…reimposing sanctions under a policy of maximum pressure…

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:44.880
…once again raising tensions in the region

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:47.120
It convinced the Iranian hardliners…

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:50.600
…that you can never trust America’s words

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:51.880
People really did suffer…

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:53.880
…and this gave America tremendous leverage

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:56.600
But Donald Trump didn’t really use this leverage to any end

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:59.520
In fact, by the end of Donald Trump’s term…

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.080
…Iran was closer to a bomb than at the start of it

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:05.160
President Biden has already indicated…

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.440
…he would like to rejoin the JCPOA

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:12.280
The only way out of this crisis is through diplomacy

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:15.040
But the road ahead is far from certain

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:18.200
Iran will elect a new president in 2021…

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:22.560
…who is widely expected to be more hardline than his predecessor…

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:26.840
…making any end to the feud even more unlikely

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:28.120
The irony of this rivalry…

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:31.280
…is that American society and Iranian society…

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:33.240
…are much closer to each other than, say…

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:35.880
…American society and Saudi society…

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.360
…but their leaders are held hostage by history

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:42.360
So as much as it would be somewhat natural for America and Iran…

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.480
…to have this rapprochement…

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:46.320
…it’s going to be very difficult to bring about

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:50.160
I’m Roger McShane, the Middle East editor for The Economist

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:53.040
If you want to read more of our coverage on Iran…

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:54.240
…please click the link

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:55.800
Thank you for watching…

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:57.240
…don’t forget to subscribe

